   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.14",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes",
    "stem": "Let \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{3}{x-2}\\). Which statement correctly describes the one-sided limits at \\(x=2\\) and whether the graph has a vertical asymptote there?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^-}f(x)=+\\infty\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)=-\\infty\\); \\(x=2\\) is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Left–right reversal assigns the signs to the wrong sides: x − 2 is negative when x < 2 and positive when x > 2. Fix: Test the denominator’s sign at one input on each side of 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^-}f(x)=+\\infty\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)=+\\infty\\); \\(x=2\\) is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Ignoring the denominator’s sign confuses unbounded magnitude with positive unbounded behavior. From the left, the positive numerator divided by a negative denominator gives negative values. Fix: Determine the quotient’s sign before assigning +∞ or −∞."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^-}f(x)=-\\infty\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)=+\\infty\\); \\(x=2\\) is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The denominator approaches zero through negative values from the left and positive values from the right, so the quotient grows without bound with the indicated signs. These infinite one-sided limits establish the vertical asymptote x = 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^-}f(x)=-\\infty\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)=+\\infty\\); there is no vertical asymptote at \\(x=2\\).",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Requiring matching one-sided limits incorrectly imposes a two-sided-limit condition on vertical asymptotes. A vertical asymptote exists when at least one one-sided limit is +∞ or −∞, even if the sides differ. Fix: Check each one-sided limit separately for unbounded behavior."
     }
    ]
   },
